### Bridge payout lifecycle: COMPLETED only after seal + recurring audit

- Monitor payouts now pass through a `SUBMITTED` state: the signed envelope
  and its derived hash are persisted on the deposit row *before* the first
  broadcast (ledger-first). `COMPLETED` requires the payout sealed
  `PAYOUT_SEAL_DEPTH` (2) blocks deep. An unsealed payout past
  `PAYOUT_REBROADCAST_BLOCKS` (6) is rebroadcast as the identical envelope —
  never re-signed (bounded by `PAYOUT_MAX_REBROADCAST`, 5). Apply-rejected
  payouts stay `SUBMITTED` and `ALERT`. This removes the phantom-completion
  class that stranded five deposits in the audit. `envelope_hash` tracks the
  derived hash separately from `ait_tx_hash` (server-canonical if the RPC
  normalizes differently). Wallet `bridge_db` migrated identically.
- `python -m bridge_monitor.admin`: `status`, `manual-payout` (the only
  permitted manual send path — same ledger-first flow), `abandon-and-resign`
  (frees a stuck `SUBMITTED` row only when the payout account's on-chain
  nonce has passed the envelope nonce AND the envelope hash is not sealed),
  `write-off` (terminal, requires a written reason). Rule in AGENTS.md:
  never send ad-hoc from the payout account.
- `WRITTEN_OFF` terminal state for deliberately unsettled deposits (reason
  mandatory). Deposit `0x7e1aab4b…` (0.05 Sepolia ETH, Aug-27, no recipient)
  written off: it went to the bridge-contract deployer `0x818018F3…` whose
  key was already declared lost — unrecoverable testnet funds. The row's
  phantom `completed` state is corrected to `written_off` with the reason.
- `fleet-config-check` gains a recurring bridge payout audit
  (`scripts/monitoring/bridge-payout-audit.py`): every COMPLETED row's
  `ait_tx_hash` must exist sealed on chain; every ETH deposit hash maps to
  at most one distinct payout; written-off rows are listed, not failed.
  Live run green: 9 ledger rows, 7 sealed payouts verified.
- `BRIDGE_LOW_FLOAT_ETH` (0.01) alerts when the bridge wallet cannot cover
  refund/withdrawal gas — currently firing: `0x0936…` holds 0.00066 ETH and
  needs an operator faucet top-up (captcha-gated).
- Sepolia bridge contract `0x24403CCf…` verified owned by the lost
  `0x818018F3…` deployer key — every `onlyOwner` path is permanently frozen.
  Key-split plan updated: a new CrossChainBridge deploy with an offline
  owner key is required (validator set empty, locks unused — cheap now).

### Dead bridge contract swept + write-off guard hardened (v0.25.8)

- Frozen contract `0x24403CCff489D9355A534D34d4F88bC5b3EcF6FA`
  (`owner()` = deployer key declared unrecoverable 2026-08-31) swept
  from active config: hub's `BRIDGE_CONTRACT_ADDRESS` commented out,
  `contracts/deployments-bridge-sepolia.json` marked deprecated, the
  exchange test fixture and v0.4.14 release doc annotated, and
  `fleet-config-check.sh` FAILs on any non-commented env reference.
  Contract holds 0 ETH; it can never be paused, so the new-contract
  deploy in the key-split plan is the only safe path.
- `write-off` now refuses recipient-bearing rows (money owed) unless
  `--unpaid-with-recipient` + a reason is passed; `abandon-and-resign`
  keeps its nonce proof. `bridge-payout-audit.py` lists
  recipient-bearing write-offs separately as `WRITTEN-OFF DEBT` so an
  unpaid debt can't quietly masquerade as an unrecoverable row.

### Deposit-scanner liveness + probe retry (v0.25.8)

- `bridge-monitor` now emits `ALERT` after 3 consecutive
  `_poll_ethereum` failures — a dead Sepolia endpoint previously
  "found nothing" forever with no signal (cursor-based stall was
  invisible).
- `fleet-config-check.sh` gains a liveness section: where
  `aitbc-bridge-monitor` is active, its `bridge_cursor`
  `last_processed_block` must stay within `BRIDGE_CURSOR_MAX_LAG`
  (default 100, ~20 min) of the Sepolia head; FAILs fold into the
  bridge gate. Endpoint inventory: monitor + coordinator-api ride
  `ethereum-sepolia-rpc.publicnode.com`; exchange (hub/hub1) and
  hub1 coordinator keep the keyed Infura URL — verified live
  (head `0xb43108`), kept as the paid-key tier.
- Every ssh probe in the checker now retries once via `sshr()` —
  a single transient blip no longer paints a host UNREACHABLE and
  turns a clean run red.

### Operator float funding — FUNDING ledger state (v0.25.8)

- `BRIDGE_FUNDING_SOURCES` (comma-separated senders): ETH to the
  bridge wallet from a whitelisted sender is recorded as `FUNDING` —
  a ledger row, no payout, no ALERT. Intended for exactly one
  offline-held operator funding wallet; never a shared public faucet
  (a whitelisted faucet would silently never pay anyone who points
  it at the bridge). Unlisted senders keep today's path: no
  recipient -> FAILED + ALERT + deliberate write-off.
- `bridge-payout-audit.py` lists `funding` rows (amount + sender) so
  every ETH inflow is visible; `FUNDING` is terminal for dedup.
- `fleet-config-check.sh` reports the knob in the payout-config readout.
- hub's `aitbc-bridge-monitor.env` documents the knob (empty pending
  the operator wallet); the 0.05-ETH top-up row was written off with
  reason per policy.

### fix(bridge): serialize payouts, auto-abandon stale envelopes, record dust

- Serialize in-flight payouts: at most one SUBMITTED envelope at a time (same-poll envelopes collided on the payout-account nonce since it is read from committed chain state; the stale envelope then burned the whole rebroadcast budget awaiting manual re-sign). Deferred deposits keep their computed AIT amount and do not spend the retry budget.
- The submitted-payout sweep now runs the abandon-and-resign proof automatically: payout-account chain nonce past the envelope nonce + envelope hash unsealed -> the row is re-queued for a fresh ledger-first payout instead of rebroadcasting a dead envelope until the counter runs out.
- Nonce-slot rejections alert immediately at initial submit, retry, and rebroadcast, rather than waiting for max_rebroadcasts.
- poll_ethereum no longer advances the block cursor when both process_deposit and the retry fallback fail for a deposit; the next poll rescans the block.
- Sub-minimum ETH deposits are recorded as FAILED ledger rows (dust inflow; deliberate resolution) so the payout audit can account for every inflow.
- AIT recipients are validated at parse time with the shared valid_ait_recipient predicate (storage.py; admin.py delegates to it); malformed recipients fail permanently at once instead of burning the 5-attempt retry budget.
- The retry queue guards per-row build failures so one deposit exception cannot kill the remaining rows.

### fix(bridge): alert on payout head-of-line blocking, clear stale retry time

- Serialization trades nonce collisions for head-of-line blocking: one SUBMITTED row that can neither seal nor abandon blocks every payout queued behind it forever. _check_queue_health (per poll) ALERTs when the oldest SUBMITTED row exceeds BRIDGE_STUCK_PAYOUT_BLOCKS (default rebroadcast_blocks x max_rebroadcasts) and when PENDING_RETRY depth reaches BRIDGE_QUEUE_ALERT_DEPTH (default 5); the alert names the row and the release path.
- update_deposit gains clear_fields (allowlisted columns set to NULL): abandon_payout now clears next_retry_at, so a re-queued deposit is due immediately instead of waiting out a stale backoff (previously next_retry_at=None was silently a no-op).
- AGENTS.md documents the release matrix for a blocked row: abandon-and-resign for nonce-passed unsealed envelopes; write-off (+ manual-payout when still owed) for sealed-failed or lost rows.

### feat(v9): shadow-window positive control + scripted pin gate

- v9_signature_verdict now counts every evaluation into v9_shadow_checked_total plus per-type v9_shadow_checked series; both the checked aggregate and v9_would_reject_total are pre-created at import. A clean shadow window is provably checked>0 AND would_reject==0 instead of relying on a lazily-created series being absent.
- fleet-config-check gains a "v9 shadow window (pin gate)" section: the fleet window is bounded by the latest validator restart, reports per-host checked/reject and the per-type traffic mix, and prints WINDOW elapsed-vs-24h so the pin gate is a command output, not manual judgement.
- NOTE: v9_policy.py is chain code — it must ride the pin deploy; a fleet restart inside the window would restart the window. A checkout pull is inert (running processes keep loaded code); do NOT restart aitbc-blockchain-node until the pin.

### fix(v9): bound shadow-metric cardinality; pin gate checks the _other series

- The per-type `v9_shadow_checked_<type>_total` series are clamped to the known transaction-type allowlist — an unknown or malformed `tx_type` can no longer mint unbounded Prometheus label series (`091ca4c`). The shadow-window bound is anchored to validator restarts only.
- `IPFS_SUBSCRIPTION` joined `V9_METRIC_KNOWN_TX_TYPES` — it was landing in `_other` despite being a legitimate type. New `test_known_metric_types_cover_apply_path` derives the apply-recognized type set mechanically and fails on drift (`7fdf202`).
- The fleet-check pin gate prints `v9_shadow_checked_other_total` per host and flags any non-zero value — a clean window requires it to be 0 or explained on record (`22a486d`).
- Restart epochs are read via `ActiveEnterTimestamp` + `date -d` — the fleet's systemd build lacks USec (`f120261`).

### chore(fleet-check): identical-checkout line + effective transition heights

- The validator section prints whether all validator checkouts sit on the same commit (`2dae537`).
- A new "effective transition heights" section reads each node's `/proc/<pid>/environ` — catching env files, `Environment=` lines and systemd drop-ins — falls back to the checkout's code defaults when a var is unset, and flags both V4–V9 env shadowing and cross-host effective drift (`0267c82`).

### docs(governance): votes are advisory (V-7)

- Governance votes submitted over RPC are advisory tallies — a vote row is written only on the node that received the RPC, never propagated or applied. `docs/governance/01-ARCHITECTURE.md` now says so; feature-status pages claiming on-chain vote submission are stale (`8b96dc8`).

### feat(bridge): BR-7 payout hardening — price lock, per-deposit caps, committed float

- The first computed `ait_amount` is locked on the row: a retry pays exactly it, and a recompute only runs when the row was never priced — including the `amount_ait='0'` case the old `not ait_amount` check treated as priced, which could have submitted a 0-AIT payout after an oracle outage (`974858a`).
- Per-deposit caps: a payout may not exceed `BRIDGE_MAX_PAYOUT_FRACTION` (default 0.5) of the payout wallet nor, when set, the absolute `BRIDGE_MAX_PAYOUT_AIT` ceiling — the stricter binds (`5e6f7a8`); the absolute ceiling still applies when the balance RPC is unreadable (`551f068`). Over-cap deposits land FAILED with an operator alert; `manual-payout` remains the deliberate, uncapped override.
- The low-float alert counts *available* float — balance minus amounts committed to non-terminal rows — not the raw balance.
- `fleet-config-check.sh` reports `BRIDGE_MAX_PAYOUT_FRACTION`, `BRIDGE_MAX_PAYOUT_AIT`, and `BRIDGE_FUNDING_SOURCES` per host.

### docs(readme/join): welcome-page README; join docs name the real island

- README is now a welcome page — role diagram, key features, doc pointers — with setup/join/job commands delegated to the docs that carry them (`40ab626`, `737906`).
- The public island is named: join-facing docs carry `hub.aitbc.bubuit.net`, chain `ait-hub.aitbc.bubuit.net`, island `ait-hub.aitbc.bubuit.net-island`, and `http://` (no TLS listener is live — peer keys over `/rpc/join` are cleartext until then). Generic `hub.example.net` uses in API docs and scenarios are unchanged (`020a98`).

