### feat(db): opt-in keeper connection (DB_KEEPER_CONNECTION) — stops the close-time WAL checkpoint stalls

- Why: on 2026-10-01 node2's v9 attestation check hit `database is locked` at height 30415 and declined to attest (fail-closed), and its propose loop logged repeated `Failed to propose block: database is locked`. A `/proc/locks` sampler on node2 (10:03-10:28Z) found 32 holds of 2 s or more, all by node2's own RPC and node processes and none by an outside process; the 10:07:32Z failure sat inside an 11.5 s EXCLUSIVE hold by the RPC process.
- Mechanism: every session opens its own connection (NullPool), so whenever none is open the closing one is the last, and SQLite checkpoints and deletes the WAL under an EXCLUSIVE lock while it syncs the disk. node2's WAL is deleted and recreated several times per 30 s. node0/node1/node2 are containers on one machine with chain.db on btrfs over spinning disks: a 4 KB write plus fsync takes a median of ~220 ms with outliers over 1.7 s, against 2 ms on hub and hub1 (ext4). A connection opened during the exclusive hold waits Python's default 5 s (the 60 s busy timeout is set only after the first pragma) and fails.
- Change: `settings.db_keeper_connection` (env `DB_KEEPER_CONNECTION`, default off). When on, `init_db` opens one idle connection per chain and `shutdown_db` closes it, so no session is ever the last one: the WAL stays on disk, is only checkpointed passively, and the exclusive lock is never taken. Failing to open it only logs a warning. Scratch check: with the last connection closed the WAL is deleted; with an idle keeper open it persists.
- Operational note: while it is on, `chain.db` alone is not the whole database; copy it with `sqlite3 .backup` or with the node stopped, never a bare `cp`, and open only `.backup` copies with `immutable=1` (replay-chain already requires a copy).
- Tests: `apps/blockchain-node/tests/test_db_keeper.py` (7 cases; the four behaviour tests fail with the `init_db` hook removed). Rollout is per host by env flag, node0 first; the bounded attestation retry from `a5b72f4605` covers holds up to ~10 s and is a partial mitigation next to this.

### ops(followers): reset scripts survive a WAL left on disk

- `reset-follower-to-snapshot.sh` installed the new `chain.db` with `mv -f` and never removed the old `chain.db-wal` / `chain.db-shm`. SQLite replays a leftover WAL onto whatever file it is next to, so a follower whose old process had been killed (or whose host runs with `DB_KEEPER_CONNECTION`, which keeps the WAL on disk by design) ended up with a corrupt hybrid. Scratch reproduction: 1,916 rows of mixed snapshot, old and old-WAL data where 3,000 were expected, and a failing `integrity_check`. The stale `-wal`/`-shm` are now removed before the swap (the genesis script already did this).
- Both reset scripts made their pre-reset safety copy with a bare `cp -p` of `chain.db`, which drops commits that exist only in the WAL. It now goes through `sqlite3 .backup`, with a raw copy that keeps the `-wal`/`-shm` files as the fallback.
- `tests/test_reset_follower_scripts.py` runs both scripts for real against a temp data dir (systemctl, ssh, rsync, chown and install stubbed): the installed database is clean and the safety copy holds the WAL-only rows. Two of its five cases fail against the previous scripts. The nightly `aitbc-backup.sh` (`sqlite3 .dump`) and `backfill-follower-gap.sh` were checked and need no change.

### perf(bridge): stop rewriting every bridge_block_header row on every block

- Found while checking node0's WAL after the keeper rollout (2026-10-01): 99.8% of the live WAL frames (3,182 of 3,189, nearly all distinct pages) were `bridge_block_header`. `BridgeFinalityMixin._increment_confirmations` bumped `confirmation_count` on every earlier header at every new header, so each block rewrote the whole table: 22,267 rows, 12.5 MiB, ~3,200 WAL frames per block on every node (all five hold the same table; only 6 rows were still unfinalized), growing with chain height. At the current ~68 s block interval (about 1,260 blocks a day) that is ~15 GB/day of WAL writes per node, and every close-time checkpoint copies those ~3,200 pages under the exclusive lock instead of a handful. How much of the node2 `database is locked` stalls that explains has not been measured yet.
- Change: counting stops at `bridge_confirmation_count_cap` (default 100, never below `bridge_finality_blocks` or `bridge_min_confirmations`). Nothing decides on a count above the deepest requirement (`_check_finality_for_transfer` needs at most `bridge_finality_blocks`, the flag keys off it), and 100 keeps the reported depth accurate for SDK callers with their own, deeper `FinalityConfig`. Rows already past the cap at rollout (counts up to ~22k) keep their frozen value; nothing reads them. The finality-flag UPDATE now runs even when nothing was bumped.
- Measured on a scratch table built to the live shape (22,267 rows, 3,189 pages, 12.5 MiB): one new header wrote 3,191 WAL frames (12.46 MiB) before and 25 frames (0.10 MiB) after. The live WAL showed 3,189 frames for a block, which is the cross-check.
- Visible effect: bridge RPC `confirmation_count` stops at the cap (100) for headers older than that; finality decisions are unchanged. Bridge locks are paused fleet-wide (`BRIDGE_LOCKS_PAUSED=true`), and `BRIDGE_RELEASE_ENABLED=true` is set on all five nodes, so new headers start at 0 and count up only through this loop; a release needs at most `bridge_finality_blocks` (6) confirmations, which the cap never undercuts. `bridge_block_header` is a per-node cache classed `service` in the state digest, so there is no consensus or digest effect and nodes can take it on rolling restarts.
- Tests: `tests/test_bridge_confirmation_cap.py` (13 cases; the behavioural ones run with the release fence up, as on the fleet, and down; 11 fail on the old code). The 12 existing test files that touch the bridge header code (147 tests, including `test_bridge_confirmation_bump_equivalence.py`, which still pins the values below the cap against the row loop) pass unchanged with the repo's conftest.

