### ops(k1): authority rotation executed on-chain at 30551-30564 — governance_executors→0x04fE… (add+prove+remove, old key rejected unsealed at propose), bridge_release_authority→0x2b0F… (fresh dedicated key), escrow_settlement_authority→0x03DF…; ESCROW_RELEASE_*/BRIDGE_RELEASE_* env deployed fleet-wide, rpc+event-bridge restarted


### fix(rpc): refuse authority-gated types from the wrong sender at mempool admission (V-8)

- Found 1 Oct during the K-1 executor rotation: a GOVERNANCE_EXECUTE from the retired executor `0x02B8…` was admitted to the mempool and only dropped when hub proposed it (`Failed to apply transaction … is not an authorized executor`, 14:21:54 CEST). Nothing was written, but each such transaction costs a mempool slot and a proposer cycle, and any funded account can send one. `_validate_transaction_admission` is the one door for REST, gossip ingest and the p2p transport, and it checked chain, sender, balance and nonce, not who may send a type.
- New `state/admission_authority.py::sender_authority_error` mirrors the two sender gates of `validate_transaction` (GOVERNANCE_EXECUTE against `governance_executors`; ESCROW_RELEASE and ESCROW_REFUND against `escrow_settlement_authority`): the same parameter lookups, version rules (fail-closed from v5, lenient below) and messages, evaluated for the version and height of the next block. The admission function calls it last, so every earlier rejection keeps its message. `validate_transaction` and the apply path are untouched.
- Not covered: BOND_SLASH, whose authority check lives in the apply function and not in `validate_transaction`.
- Behaviour to know: a transaction from a newly added executor or settlement authority is refused at admission until the parameter-changing transaction has sealed, where apply could have accepted it later in the same block. The K-1 rotation already waited for each seal.
- Tests: `tests/test_admission_authority.py`, 57 cases. With only the hook removed the five refusal cases fail; a 45-case matrix (five block versions, three types, three parameter states, signed transactions) compares the helper with `validate_transaction` and fails on any disagreement in outcome or message. The whole `apps/blockchain-node/tests` suite run the way CI does gives the same 6 failures on the parent and on the patch (4 in `test_fleet_digest_spec.py`, 1 in `test_v8_stamped_version.py`, 1 in `security/test_state_transition.py`, all present before) and 57 more passes. Not deployed.

### feat(ops): key-audit lists backup copies and finds a key by value

- On 1 Oct the retired hub key `0x02B8…` turned up in 33 backup files across the fleet, 10 of them written that day by the rotation. `scripts/ops/key-audit.py` had not shown them: it read `/etc/aitbc/*.env` only and recognised a key by its variable name, so even a name-based look at the backups missed two hub files.
- Backup-like files in `/etc/aitbc` (`*.bak*`, `*.orig`, `*.old`, `*~`, `*.save`, `*.swp`) are now listed with `backup: true` and kept out of `ok` and `mismatches`, which the nightly backup (`aitbc-backup.sh`) acts on.
- `--find-address ADDRESS` (repeatable) finds a key by value: every file in `/etc/aitbc` and the wallet and keystore directories holding a 64-hex token that derives to ADDRESS is listed with its mode, whether it is a backup and the number of occurrences, whatever the variable name, JSON field or comment around it. The key is never printed, stored or returned. Run read-only on all five hosts after the redaction it reproduced the by-value scan exactly: the old key in four files on hub (three env files and the default wallet), the default wallet on hub1, nothing elsewhere, no backups.
- `tests/test_key_audit.py` (17 cases) covers the verdict, the backup flag, by-value matching under any name, a clean redacted backup, and that no report or console output contains the key. The script gained `build_report()` so it can be tested; the report format the nightly flow reads is unchanged.

### fix(monitoring): the Prometheus alert watcher reports resolutions and refires and keeps an events-only log (SD-2)

- On 1 Oct two `ServiceDown` alerts for the stopped node exporters on hub and hub1 had been firing for three days with nobody told. The watcher (`aitbc prometheus alerts --watch`) reported an alert once per process lifetime, so a refire after a resolve was silent; it never reported that an alert had stopped; and its only file trace sat among the one-per-poll httpx lines of the service log, about 1.4 MB a day.
- It now tracks alert state across polls and emits `prometheus_alert_firing` when an alert starts firing, again if it had resolved, and `prometheus_alert_resolved` (with `duration_seconds`) when it stops. A poll Prometheus does not answer changes nothing: `_prometheus_get` returns `{}` on an outage, which used to be indistinguishable from "no alerts" and would now have read as everything resolved.
- New `--alert-log PATH` appends the same JSON lines to a file of their own, rotated at 5 MiB with 5 backups; an unwritable path degrades to the journal and the service log with one warning. The repo unit `scripts/monitoring/aitbc-prometheus-watch.service` passes `--alert-log /var/log/aitbc/alerts.log`, inside its `ReadWritePaths`. The live unit on hub needs the same line and a `daemon-reload`; that is not applied yet. The generated MCP tool for this command (`aitbc_prometheus_alerts`, `mcp-server/aitbc_mcp_cli_tools_generated_prometheus.py`) is regenerated and gains `alert_log_path`.
- `tests/test_prometheus_alert_watch.py`, 12 cases. With the old once-per-lifetime logic restored and the new option kept, 8 fail (resolve, refire, label sets, outage then clear, and the log and `--no-emit` cases that depend on them). Not deployed.


### feat(monitoring): the alert watcher pushes webhooks, takes silences, reads its own log and reports its own health

- Follow-up to SD-2: the watcher emitted events to a file nobody reads. `--notify-url URL` (repeatable, or space-separated `AITBC_WATCH_NOTIFY_URL`) POSTs every event record as JSON — `prometheus_watch_started`, `prometheus_alert_firing`, `prometheus_alert_still_firing`, `prometheus_alert_resolved` — with one retry, 5 s timeout, and failures counted in `aitbc_prometheus_watch_notify_errors_total` instead of ever killing the loop.
- `--silence-file PATH` (`AITBC_WATCH_SILENCE_FILE`) reloads a JSON silence list every poll: `{"match": {"alertname": ..., "labels": {...}}}` subset matching, optional ISO `until`, free-text `reason`. A silenced transition is still written to the alert log with `silenced: true` — the push is muted, the record is not — and an alert whose silence ends while it still fires re-emits as `via: "silence_expired"`. Empty matches silence nothing; a malformed file fails open. Permanently retired scrape targets should still be removed at the source node's scrape config rather than silenced forever.
- New `aitbc prometheus alert-history` replays `/var/log/aitbc/alerts.log` (or `--path` for a rotated file) with `--since 24h`, `--alertname`, `--node`, `--state firing|resolved|silenced`, `--last N`, and reports `firing_now`, reconstructed from the full stream so a resolve outside the window still clears its alert.
- `--remind-interval SECONDS` re-emits a still-firing alert on a cadence; silenced alerts are not reminded and the cadence restarts after a Prometheus outage rather than bursting.
- `--metrics-file PATH` (`AITBC_WATCH_METRICS_FILE`) writes a node-exporter textfile every poll — poll timestamp, `prometheus_reachable`, firing and silenced counts, notify error counter — and `aitbc_rules.yml` gains `PrometheusWatchStale` (heartbeat absent or older than 5 min), so a dead watcher is itself an alert. Needs the textfile dir group-writable for `aitbc` (`chgrp aitbc … && chmod 2775`; fleet nodes lack `setfacl`) and a `ReadWritePaths` drop-in for the unit; the heartbeat lands mode 644 so the `prometheus` user can read it.
- The unit gains `EnvironmentFile=-/etc/aitbc/prometheus-watch.env` for per-node tuning without edits and `WatchdogSec=75` with a per-poll `systemd-notify WATCHDOG=1` so a hung loop restarts, not just a crash. Every event record now carries `via` (`startup`/`transition`/`silence_expired`/`reminder`) and `timestamp_unix` alongside the ISO field.
- `tests/test_prometheus_alert_watch.py`: 19 new cases (31 total) covering notify fan-out and failure, silence match/expiry/removal fail-open, reminders, heartbeat writes, alert-history filters and firing-now replay, and the unit file's env/watchdog lines. `aitbc_rules_test.yml` covers `PrometheusWatchStale` both arms; `promtool check rules` and `promtool test rules` pass. MCP tool file regenerated (`aitbc_prometheus_alert_history` + new `alerts` params).
- Latent bug fixed on the way: `cli_gap_analysis.py` put the repo root on `sys.path` but never `cli/`, so `import aitbc_cli` resolved through the editable install and always described the primary checkout. `generate_cli_docs.py --check` could never pass a CLI change committed from a worktree (the sync test sees the worktree via conftest, the generator saw `/opt/aitbc`). It now inserts `REPO/cli` too, so both halves of `check-cli-docs` describe the tree actually being committed.


### Hub alerts page and explorer alert endpoints

- `apps/blockchain-explorer/routers/alerts.py` (new): `GET /api/alerts` serves firing/pending alerts from the local Prometheus (`PROMETHEUS_URL`, default `http://127.0.0.1:9090`), merges watcher `silenced` state from the event-log replay, reports the watcher textfile heartbeat age, and falls back to the log replay (`"source": "event_log"`) when Prometheus is down. `GET /api/alerts/history` replays `/var/log/aitbc/alerts.log` (`AITBC_ALERT_LOG`) newest-first with `limit`/`alertname`/`node`/`state` filters matching `aitbc prometheus alert-history`.
- `website/alerts.html` + `alerts.js`: public "Network Alerts" page — monitor-status strip (Prometheus reachability, watcher heartbeat staleness), firing/pending cards with severity badges and silence tags, and the recent-event table; auto-refreshes every 30 s. Reached through the existing `/explorer-api/` nginx location; data is fleet-wide on the hub because nodes remote-write into its Prometheus.
- Nav `Alerts` link added to all pages; sitemap, llms.txt, and the website README endpoint table updated; monitoring-setup.md documents the endpoints and env knobs.


### Journal errors reach the alerts page — counter timer, rule, and read endpoint

- `scripts/monitoring/journal-errors-textfile.py` + `aitbc-journal-errors.{service,timer}`: every 5 min, counts warning-and-worse journal entries per unit over a rolling 15-min window into `aitbc_journal.prom` (`aitbc_journal_error_messages{unit}`, `aitbc_journal_warning_messages{unit}`, plus scan health gauges). Two journald signals are merged because aitbc services log through stdout at journald PRIORITY=6 — the true level lives in the message's `[WARNING]`/`[ERROR]`/`[CRITICAL]` prefix: `journalctl -p err` catches real-priority records (watchdog kills, unit failures) and `journalctl -g` catches our services' text levels, deduplicated on the journal cursor. Runs as `aitbc` with `SupplementaryGroups=systemd-journal` — no /etc/group edit needed.
- `aitbc_rules.yml` gains `AITBCJournalErrors` (`aitbc_journal_error_messages > 0` for 5 min, warning severity): any unit logging an error alerts until the window slides past it.
- `routers/journal.py`: `GET /api/journal/recent?unit&priority&limit&since_minutes` — journalctl rows restricted to `aitbc-*` units in code (validated `unit` param + post-fetch filter), so the public page can show "what did the service actually log" without exposing sshd/kernel/system entries. Explorer unit gains `SupplementaryGroups=systemd-journal` for the read access.
- alerts.html gains a Service Journal card: unit + priority selectors over a warnings-and-errors table, refreshed with the rest of the page.

### Round-1 proposal log demoted to INFO

- `poa.py`: the "height has gone N rounds" proposal message now logs WARNING only at round >= 2. Round 1 is the documented landing spot for every empty heartbeat block in an idle hybrid chain (the 60s idle gate ends round 0 exactly), so it warned on 100% of empty blocks — pure noise on the new alerts page journal card. Round >=2 is the real signal: scheduled owner and first fallback both silent.

### Journal collector gets a stale-watchdog rule

- `AITBCJournalScanStale` in `aitbc_rules.yml`: fires when `aitbc_journal_scan_timestamp_seconds` is absent or older than 10 min for 5 min — a dead `aitbc-journal-errors.timer` would otherwise freeze `aitbc_journal_error_messages` and silently blind `AITBCJournalErrors`. promtool tests cover the frozen-series and absent arms; `promtool check`/`test rules` pass (26 rules).
